  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e12a853bf999e4844889628620e02db2, type: 3}
  m_Name: LogTextDatabase
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::LogTextDatabase
  logEntries:
  - message: 0
    logTitle: 'Log 001: Routine Check'
    logDate: Sol 003
    logText: 'Author: Commander Elias Ward.

      Timestamp: Sol 3.


      Everything
      is functioning within acceptable parameters. Oxygen recyclers are stable, power
      draw is minimal, and the weather is clear. Command keeps reminding me that
      this mission is historic. The first long-term solo rotation on the south ridge.
      I suppose it is.


      The silence out here is different from training. Back
      on Earth, silence always had something behind it. Wind, traffic, distant voices.
      Here it''s just... empty. The vacuum presses against the walls, like the whole
      moon is leaning in to listen.


      Still, nothing to report. I''ve started
      organizing the storage bay to pass the time. Whoever replaces me will appreciate
      the effort.


      I keep catching myself talking out loud. Just to hear something.


      Perfectly
      normal, I think.'
    isDiscovered: 1
  - message: 1
    logTitle: 'Log 002: Minor Anomaly'
    logDate: Sol 011
    logText: 'Author: Commander Elias Ward.

      Timestamp: Sol 11.


      Something
      odd today. Not a malfunction. just... misplaced equipment.


      I left a
      wrench on the diagnostics console before heading to the airlock. When I returned,
      it was on the floor near the storage lockers. No damage, no sign of impact.
      Just sitting there like it had always been there.


      I checked the camera
      logs. Nothing. No movement recorded.


      Probably my fault. Fatigue does
      strange things in low gravity. You put something down lightly and it drifts
      farther than you expect.


      Still, I''ve started double checking where
      I leave things.

      And locking the interior doors at night.


      Not
      because I''m worried.

      Just being thorough.'
    isDiscovered: 1
  - message: 2
    logTitle: 'Log 003: Visual Disturbance'
    logDate: Sol 019
    logText: 'Author: Commander Elias Ward.

      Timestamp: Sol 19.


      I saw
      someone outside on the ridge today.


      At first I thought it was a trick
      of the visor. Maybe light scattering across the dust. But the shape held together.
      Tall. Upright. Standing perfectly still on the horizon.


      No suit transponder.
      No shuttle signatures. No scheduled arrivals.


      When I turned to retrieve
      the binoculars, it was gone.


      I reviewed the external cameras afterward.
      Empty terrain. Nothing unusual.


      I''m logging this as a visual misinterpretation
      caused by fatigue and isolation. That''s the rational explanation.


      But
      I can''t shake the feeling that it was looking directly at me.'
    isDiscovered: 1
  - message: 3
    logTitle: 'Log 004: Cognitive Degradation'
    logDate: Sol 026
    logText: 'Author: Commander Elias Ward.

      Timestamp: Sol 26.


      I keep
      losing time.


      Not hours. just minutes. Small gaps. I''ll start a task,
      blink, and suddenly it''s finished. Tools already put away. Systems already
      checked. No memory of doing it.


      Today I found footprints in the dust
      outside the habitat. They matched my boots exactly. Same tread pattern. Same
      stride.


      The problem is...

      I hadn''t left the airlock.


      I
      checked the logs three times. No airlock activity. No depressurization alarms.


      I
      don''t trust the cameras anymore. They show what they want to show.


      Something
      is moving when I''m not looking.

      I know it is.'
    isDiscovered: 1
  - message: 4
    logTitle: 'Log 005: Final Entry'
    logDate: Unknown
    logText: 'Author: Commander Elias Ward.

      Timestamp: Unknown.


      They''re
      inside now.


      Not in the base. Not exactly.


      In the corners of
      my vision.

      In the reflections on the glass.

      Standing just behind
      me when I turn around.


      I stopped sleeping because that''s when they
      get closer.


      I tried to leave the habitat this morning, but the airlock
      wouldn''t open. Or maybe I forgot how to use it. I keep forgetting things.
      Simple things. Words. Procedures. My own voice sounds unfamiliar.


      The
      worst part is the silence.


      It isn''t empty anymore.


      It''s crowded.


      They''re
      waiting for me to notice them all at once.

      I think that''s when it ends.


      If
      anyone finds this log...

      do not trust what you see out there.


      It
      is not alone.

      And soon... neither will you.'
    isDiscovered: 1
